{
  "text": "People think the hardest part of building a startup is the idea. It isn't. I spent seven years building a company that almost died because of one spreadsheet mistake. We had twelve thousand dollars left. I opened the bank account and the number was negative forty thousand. My co-founder said, we're done. Then I found the error. Our payment processor had charged every customer twice for six months. We refunded everyone, and the next morning we had more cash than the day we launched. The lesson? Most startup emergencies are just accounting errors with better marketing. But the strangest part happened a week later. A customer emailed to thank us for the refund and asked if we could charge him again because he wanted to support the company. That single email raised more money than our first investor. Now let me tell you about the worst hiring decision I ever made. I hired a brilliant engineer who refused to talk to customers. For eight months he built exactly what we asked for, and exactly none of what they needed. The product looked perfect in every demo. In real life, people opened it, stared at it, and closed it. Our retention dropped from forty percent to eleven percent in one quarter. I finally sat next to a customer for three hours and watched her use the thing. She never once clicked the button we were most proud of. She used a spreadsheet instead. That afternoon I deleted half the product. The team thought I had lost my mind. Revenue went up nineteen percent the following month because the remaining half was actually useful. Here is the counterintuitive part: the feature we deleted was the one investors had praised the most. They were not the user. They were a different audience with a different job to be done. If you only remember one thing, remember this. Your smartest employee can still be pointed at the wrong problem. Proximity to the customer beats intelligence in the room. The best advice I ever got came from a janitor in our first office. He said, you keep moving the furniture around, but the door is still locked. It took me two years to understand what he meant.",
